      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "In 22 patients with typical chest pain and normal coronary arteries (microvascular angina, syndrome X) 99mTc-MIBI-SPECT was examined in regard to assess impairment of myocardial perfusion reserve."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "The study was performed with 99mTc-MIBI-SPECT at rest and under vasodilation with dipyridamole. The findings were compared with a normal database. A normal perfusion reserve was said to be an increase > 20% of the 99mTc-MIBI-activity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In 2/22 (9%) of the patients the perfusion reserve lay > 20% i.e. 37%. In 91% of the patients a diminution or even decrease of the perfusion was to be seen. From these 9/22 (41%) of the patients showed a diminution of the 99mTc-MIBI-uptake by 6%. 1/22 patients had a decrease of the perfusion under vasodilation with dipyridamole i.e. a lower activity of 99mTc-MIBI by 13%"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Vasodilation 99mTc-MIBI-SPECT offers good imaging quality and enables semi-quantitative assessment of myocardial perfusion reserve in patients with microvascular angina."
        }
      ]},
